  "query_preview": "..Today will be similar to yesterday with high\npressure still in place over the southwest and temperatures 3 to\n10 degrees above normal. Just enough moisture will be in place\nfor an isolated storm or two over the White Mountains as well as\nCochise County. Winds will also be easterly with some breezes in\nthe Gila River Valley through Saturday. Friday the ridge begins\nto weaken some as a small closed low ahead of a larger Pacific \ntrough moves south along the California coast. This is what will\ncause Hurricane Rachel to begin its northeastward turn towards \nBaja. Temperatures Friday will be similar to today with another\nslight chance for storms in the White Mounatins. The 08/06Z HRRR \nis also showing isolated storms from Tucson westward, likely \ninitiated from early moisture advection. PWATs could be as high \nas 1.25\" by Friday afternoon. However, confidence in this storm\nactivity is low given it's only the HRRR showing this and no \nother HREF or REFS members. \n\nMuch better and more widespread rain chances begin this weekend\ninto early next week as tropical moisture interacts with a\nPacific trough. Saturday into early next week a Pacific trough\nsets up over the West Coast. At first moving inland over the\nPacific Northwest then digging south and absorbing the\naforementioned small closed low by Monday. Rachel is expected \nto weaken to a tropical storm in the next few hours, and will \nlikely be post-tropical when its remnants make landfall late \nSaturday or early Sunday morning. The current NHC track has \nRachel making landfall over northern Baja California, then \nmoving up the lower Colorado River Valley. This will lead to an \nincrease in moisture for southeast Arizona beginning Saturday \nwith PWATs around 200 percent of normal. Expect scattered \nmoderate showers Saturday and Sunday with some embedded \nthunderstorms. The heaviest rain at this time looks to be a \nlittle further north over central Arizona. The trough will also \nbring breezy to windy conditions Sunday and Monday afternoons \nwith speeds 15-30 mph and gusts to 40 mph. Winds will be \nstrongest on Sunday. \n\nRachel will not be the only tropical system to watch over the\nnext few days. Newly named Simon is currently centered a couple\nhundred miles west/southwest of Acapulco. Over the next day or\nso it is forecasted to strengthen into a Hurricane and move\nnorthward reaching central Sinaloa late Monday night. This will\nsend re-enforcing tropical moisture towards southeast Arizona\nkeeping PWATs around 200 percent of normal through at least\nTuesday. On its current trajectory the best moisture will be\njust east in New Mexico, but it will be enough to keep rain\nchances going into Tuesday and Wednesday. \n\nCurrent forecast rainfall totals Saturday into Tuesday are\n0.25\"-1.00\" in the valleys and 1.0\"-2.0\" in the mountains. This\nis more of a long duration rain event and flash flooding is not\nexpected. But by Monday and Tuesday area washes could have some\nflow in them depending on rainfall amounts. Still some spread in\nthe QPF forecast so expect these rainfall amounts to fluctuate\nas the weekend and early next week approaches.",
